      "dom_id": "question-47794",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Solve the equation $\\frac{9}{6x-3}=\\frac{2}{3x+1}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ cannot be solutions since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nTo solve the equation, we first apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{6x-3} & =\\frac{2}{3x+1} \\\\ 9\\cdot (3x+1) & =(6x-3)\\cdot 2 \\\\ 27x+9 & =12x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}27x+9 & =12x-6 \\\\ 27x+9-12x & =12x-6-12x \\\\ 15x+9 & =-6 \\\\ 15x+9-9 & =-6-9 \\\\ 15x & =-15 \\\\ \\frac{15x}{15} & =\\frac{-15}{15} \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince  $x=-1$ is different from $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=-\\frac{1}{3},$ we can accept it as a solution.\nThus, the solution is $x=-1.$",
