      "name": null,
      "problem": "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{3x+12}=\\frac{2}{x+4}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that $x=-4$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nTo solve the equation, we first apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3x+12} & =\\frac{2}{x+4} \\\\ 2\\cdot (x+4) & =(3x+12)\\cdot 2 \\\\ 2x+8 & =6x+24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+8 & =6x+24 \\\\ 2x+8-24 & =6x+24-24 \\\\ 2x-16 & =6x \\\\ 2x-16-2x & =6x-2x \\\\ -16 & =4x \\\\ \\frac{-16}{4} & =\\frac{4x}{4} \\\\ -4 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=-4$ cannot be a solution, the equation has no solutions.",
