      "dom_id": "question-102818",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{x-1}=\\frac{2}{x+3}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that $x=-3$ and $x=1$ cannot be solutions since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nTo solve the equation, we first apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-1} & =\\frac{2}{x+3} \\\\ 1\\cdot (x+3) & =(x-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\ x+3 & =2x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3 & =2x-2 \\\\ x+3-x & =2x-2-x \\\\ 3 & =x-2 \\\\ 3+2 & =x-2+2 \\\\ 5 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=5$ is different from $x=-3$ and $x=1,$ we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x=5.$",
